body:
`GLOBAL_GET` is an expensive operation which should not be used each frame / tick.
This PR adds macros which do a cheaper revision check, and only call the expensive `GLOBAL_GET` when project settings have changed.

Updated version of #60926

* Recent profiling revealed some cases where we were still calling `GET_GLOBAL` per frame / tick or in bottlenecks.
* Some investigation revealed the true HORROR of misuse of `GET_GLOBAL`. :scream: 
* I would like to suggest we consider following up this PR by renaming `GET_GLOBAL` to something that forces contributors / reviewers to make a conscious choice here. Something like "GET_GLOBAL_UNCACHED" would do the job. 
* This may also be a problem in 4.x, and it may be worth doing the same there if we agree on using this method.

## Timings

Very rough and ready benchmark in release build (using attribute no inline and a counter to help prevent optimizing out) gives 860 usec for the new `GLOBAL_GET_CACHED` and 99039 usec for the old `GLOBAL_GET`, so approx 115x faster on Linux.

```
	// Benchmark proj settings
	uint64_t before = OS::get_singleton()->get_ticks_usec();
	for (uint32_t n = 0; n < 1000000; n++) {
		bool was_true = GLOBAL_GET_CACHED(bool, "debug/settings/stdout/print_fps");
	}
	uint64_t after = OS::get_singleton()->get_ticks_usec();

	uint64_t before2 = OS::get_singleton()->get_ticks_usec();
	for (uint32_t n = 0; n < 1000000; n++) {
		bool was_true = GLOBAL_GET("debug/settings/stdout/print_fps");
	}
	uint64_t after2 = OS::get_singleton()->get_ticks_usec();
	print_line("GET_CACHED : " + itos(after - before) + ", GET : " + itos(after2 - before2));
```

## Discussion
This problem keeps resurfacing - that contributors do not recognise that `GET_GLOBAL` is an expensive operation, and copy other code and end up using it per frame or tick where _it should not be used_.

There are a number of mechanisms to allow code to efficiently access `ProjectSettings` (including checking `is_changed()` and using the `project_settings_changed` signal). However these involve some boiler plate and aren't as convenient for contributors on a day to day basis as just calling `GET_GLOBAL()`.

At the time @reduz hoped that users would do this properly with the required boiler plate and cache the variables themselves. I was not convinced this would happen and indeed in the 3 years since, many PRs have been written since that inappropriately use `GET_GLOBAL`.

In #60926 I proposed an efficient macro to solve this problem in 99% of circumstances where contributors did not want to implement the full boiler plate.

We identified that there was one problem with this implementation - it inadvertently allowed creating static `Variants`, which won't work for some types (like `String`) because of order of construction / destruction issues.

We solve this here by having the call site specify the storage type, and `static_assert` that it is trivial. This prevents the creation of static `Variants` and also conveniently solves the same problem in most cases.

In 3.x this seems one of the most obvious and least error prone ways of solving this issue for existing code, and we should evaluate whether this makes sense in 4.x (assuming it is also expensive to call there).


